-The figure and the steps below show the `tapestry` recipe, for a task big enough to need a discussion and a plan.
-
-1. **Dobby starts the run.**
-   The run makes the task's **worktree pair**:
-   a git checkout of the project holds the code,
-   and a checkout of the records repository beside it holds LoomYard's files.
-   Everything after that happens there, so other tasks cannot see it.
-2. **Discussion.**
-   An agent turns the board entry into a **decision record**, also called a sketch:
-   the scope, the decisions taken and what is out of scope.
-   The decision record then passes its review rounds.
-3. **Plan.**
-   An agent writes a plan as a list of cards, small units of work, each naming the code it touches by its symbols.
-   Each symbol is looked up in a syntax index of the real tree,
-   so a plan that names a function that does not exist fails before any code is written.
-   A large task's plan can first be split into parts.
-   The plan then passes its review rounds.
-4. **Implementation.**
-   `lym` groups the cards into batches by their estimated size.
-   The implementation agent reads the plan once.
-   For each batch it forks an implementer, a subagent that inherits what the agent has read.
-   `lym` runs each batch's tests and records its commits.
-5. **Code review.**
-   The run merges the parent branch into the task, so the review sees the change as it will land.
-   The whole diff then passes its review rounds.
-   The project's regression gate, if it has one, runs here as well.
-6. **Landing.**
-   An agent writes the change description, which becomes the landing commit's message.
-   Depending on the hub's settings, the task lands through a GitHub pull request or straight into its parent branch.
-   You can discuss a pull request with an agent session in the task's worktree.
-   Only one task at a time lands on a parent branch.
-7. **Cleanup.**
-   An agent reflects on what was hard or broken in LoomYard's own tooling and files it as issues against LoomYard.
-   The task's worktree pair is then torn down and the board entry is marked done.
-
-A `darn` run, for a smaller change, replaces steps 2 to 4 with one writer.
-The writer produces a short decision record and the code.
-The code review, landing and cleanup are the same.
+The `tapestry` recipe, for a task big enough to need a discussion and a plan:
+
+| Step | What happens |
+|---|---|
+| Start | Dobby makes the task's **worktree pair**: a git checkout of the project, and beside it a checkout of the **records repository** that holds LoomYard's files. |
+| Discussion | An agent turns the board entry into a **decision record**, also called a sketch: scope, decisions taken, what is left out. |
+| Plan | An agent writes cards that name the code they touch by symbol. `lym` looks each symbol up in a syntax index of the real tree, so a plan naming a function that does not exist fails before any code is written. |
+| Implementation | `lym` groups the cards into batches. One agent reads the plan once and forks an implementer per batch; `lym` runs each batch's tests and records its commits. |
+| Code review | The run merges the parent branch into the task, so the review sees the change as it will land. The project's regression gate, if it has one, runs here. |
+| Landing | An agent writes the change description, which becomes the landing commit's message. The task lands through a GitHub pull request or straight into its parent branch, one task at a time. |
+| Cleanup | An agent files what was hard or broken in LoomYard's own tooling as issues against LoomYard. The worktree pair is torn down. |
 
 The decision record, the plan and the final diff each pass **review rounds** before the run moves on.
-In each round, a fresh reviewer session reviews the artifact and a fresh fixer session fixes what it found.
-A **judge** then reads the review and the record of earlier rounds, never the artifact itself.
-The judge decides whether another round is needed.
-A fix the judge rates as mechanical is accepted without another review;
-otherwise the next round's fresh reviewer reviews the fixed artifact.
+A fresh reviewer reviews the artifact and a fresh fixer fixes what it found.
+A **judge**, who reads the review and the earlier rounds but never the artifact, decides whether another round is needed.
 The reviewer, the fixer and the judge are never the session that wrote the artifact.
 They are Claude sessions like the writer, though, so a review can share the writer's blind spots.
 Each review has a budget of rounds, by default 2 for the decision record, 3 for the plan and 1 for the code.
-A run that spends its budget without settling halts, and the orch asks you, instead of looping.
+A run that spends its budget without settling halts, and the orch asks you.
 
 At the end of each agent turn, `lym` checks the files the agent handed back.
-When they fail the check, `lym` sends the findings back into the same session.
-The run does not move on until the check passes;
-after a set number of failed attempts the run halts.
-
-A run is carried by three kinds of process.
-Dobby, a `lym` daemon with one instance per beam, decides when each run moves.
-It runs `lym batten`, the part of `lym` that walks a recipe, in short passes:
-each pass takes the run as far as it can, such as starting an agent or checking its output, then exits,
-and dobby starts the next pass when something changes.
-The agents run in terminals of their own, which the run's steps start, and the run's state lives in the task's records.
-The orch steps in only when a run halts: dobby tells it, and it fixes what it can and asks you about the rest.
-`lym batten status <slug>` shows where a run stands.
+A failed check goes back into the same session, and the run does not move on until it passes.
+After a set number of failed attempts the run halts.
+
+### Recipes
+
+| Recipe | For |
+|---|---|
+| `tapestry` | A task big enough to need a discussion and a plan. |
+| `darn` | A change one writer can make, test and commit; it replaces discussion, plan and implementation with one writer. |
+| `motif` | Settling a design in a decision record, with no code. |
+| `sampler` | A change you work out live with an agent. |
+
+A task with no recipe runs the hub's default, `darn` unless the hub changed it.
+The recipe files are data, in [`contracts/recipes/`](contracts/recipes/).
 
 ## Beyond the run
 
-1. **Mechanical gates.**
-   Tests run in tiers, from fast to slow, and only for the code a change reaches.
-   A project with numerical output can add a regression gate.
-   The gate compares its results with a baseline taken before the change.
-   The gates support Go and .NET projects today;
-   another language needs a reader for its test runner's output and a rule for which tests a change reaches.
-2. **Review lenses in forks.**
-   A reviewer can fork one subagent per lens, a focus such as missing tests or callers the change breaks.
-   A fork inherits its parent's context and prompt cache, so it starts without re-reading what the parent read.
-3. **Parallel tasks, projects and machines.**
-   Tasks run in parallel.
-   The orch makes tasks whose labels overlap run one after the other (see [The board](#the-board)).
-   A project on the board can become a beam, a long-lived branch its tasks land into.
-   The beam then lands into the main branch as one unit.
-   A run's state is pushed with the records repository,
-   so several machines can work on different projects at the same time.
-4. **Interactive agent sessions.**
-   Every agent runs in an interactive terminal as a [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code) session.
-   A session keeps its skills, hooks and subagents,
-   and you can attach to any session with `lym reed attach` and watch.
-5. **Messaging through hooks.**
-   `creel`, the hub's message service, delivers messages into running agent sessions.
-   It uses Claude Code's hooks, commands Claude Code runs at fixed points such as after each tool call.
-   For a Codex run it uses Codex's hooks in the same way.
-   The senders are the orch, other agents and `lym` itself.
-6. **A long-lived orch.**
-   A watcher clears or compacts the orch's context before the window fills,
-   so the orch session continues past one context window.
-
-Under trial: `creel` messages to Codex sessions through Codex's hooks, as part of the Codex support.
-Planned: a Slack integration for `creel`.
